Native contour metrics (src/autoseg_evaluator/vendor/)
================================================================================
Two packages computing planar contour metrics directly on RTSTRUCT polygons are
VENDORED BYTE-IDENTICAL under `src/autoseg_evaluator/vendor/`:

  native_contour_metrics       0.1.0        pure Python reference engine
  native_contour_metrics_fast  0.2.0.dev2   compiled engine (default)

They implement the definitions in Boukerroui D, Vasquez Osorio E, Brunenberg E,
Gooding MJ, "Analytic calculations and synthetic shapes for validation of
quantitative contour comparison software", Physics and Imaging in Radiation
Oncology 26 (2023) 100436, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.phro.2023.100436.

They are NOT Gooding's Chapter-15 implementation and do not reproduce it; they
follow the paper where the two differ (notably the symmetric median, which
Chapter 15 averages and the paper maximises). Both were developed for this
project and are covered by this project's Apache 2.0 licence.

Synthetic validation fixtures under `third_party/native_contour_metrics/v0.2/
data/` are derived from Boukerroui D and Gooding MJ (2022), "The Vitruvian Man
Dataset of Analytic calculations and synthetic shapes for validation of
quantitative contour comparison software", version 1, Mendeley Data,
https://doi.org/10.17632/9xjyrftzth.1, licensed CC BY 4.0
(https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/). Retained source metadata is in
`data/DATASET_ATTRIBUTION.json`. The golden values computed from them are ours
and are not author-endorsed results.

The bundled Windows library `vendor/native_contour_metrics_fast/bin/
windows-x86_64/fast_native.dll` was compiled from the C++17 source preserved at
`src/autoseg_evaluator/vendor/cpp/fast_native.cpp` with the Microsoft C++
toolchain and its static runtime (/MT); that runtime retains Microsoft's own
redistribution terms. The library is OPTIONAL — if it is absent or refused, the
application falls back to the pure-Python reference engine.

NumPy and Shapely, which both packages require at runtime, retain their own
licences and are not bundled here.
